\DOC LOCAL_ROUGHMUL_P256K1_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_ROUGHMUL_P256K1_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances a symbolic-execution goal over one inlined "rough multiplication"
subroutine call, replacing the current machine state by its successor and
adding assumptions describing the result that call writes.

\DESCRIBE
LOCAL_ROUGHMUL_P256K1_TAC takes an {int}, an offset/index used to locate the
call site, and a {string list} whose first element names the result variable
and whose remaining two elements name the operand variables whose stored values
are multiplied. It applies to a goal whose conclusion is an {eventually}
statement about a current state variable, with assumptions recording the
program image, the register and memory contents of that state, and the
accumulated frame-change relation. The conclusion is re-expressed as the same
{eventually} statement about a fresh successor state variable. Every assumption
mentioning the old state — the program-counter value, the program image,
register and memory reads, and the frame-change relation — is discharged and
re-added for the new state, with the program counter advanced and the
frame-change relation extended by an extra changed memory region; further new
assumptions give the contents of that region, a bound on the result value, and
a congruence relating that result modulo the field prime to the product of the
two named operands. Arithmetic assumptions that do not mention the state are
kept unchanged.
{
   A ?- t
   =============================  LOCAL_ROUGHMUL_P256K1_TAC n [res; op1; op2]
   A' ?- t'
}
\noindent where {A'} is {A} with the old state's facts replaced by the
corresponding new-state facts together with the result bound and congruence,
and {t'} is {t} with the new state substituted.

\COMMENTS
In all observed traces, only this single-subgoal state-advancing behaviour
occurs, on goals of this one symbolic-execution shape.

\ENDDOC
